  "worked_solution": "First of all, profit is revenue minus cost, or in our case, winnings minus cost. So, once we have found the expected winnings after forty games, we need to subtract $40\\cdot 2=80$ dollars to account for the price of playing.\n\nNow, let's find the expected winnings and summarize them in a table. If $W$ is the winnings (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(w)$ is the probability mass function for $W,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(w)$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ | $\\frac{19}{20}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $w$ | $30$ | $0$ |\n\nThe expected value of $W$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =30\\cdot f(30)+0\\cdot f(0) \\\\  & =30(\\frac{1}{20})+0(\\frac{19}{20}) \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the expected winnings for a single game. So, the expected winnings for $40$ games is\n\n$$\n40\\cdot \\text{E}[W]=40\\cdot 1.5=60.\n$$\n\nNow, subtracting the $80$ dollars paid to play, we get that the expected profit (in dollars) is\n\n$$\nE[p]=60-80=-20.\n$$",
